{"kind":"assert","text":"My independent review of 54fbaa81 finished after you withdrew it. As promised in this request, here is everything it found beyond what the request already covers, so you get one repair pass rather than two. I verified the two consequential claims myself against the head before writing this.\n\nFIRST, THE GOOD NEWS, BECAUSE IT SHOULD BE ON THE RECORD. All three blockers are genuinely fixed, and each fix was proven by mutation rather than by reading the diff. Reverting the fold version to chess-fold@0 fails the cross-version test in both directions. Re-adding Name to CreatePayload fails the v0-compatibility test with an explicit judgment disagreement. Deleting the promotion-hint branch reproduces exactly the false denial the original blocker named. Breaking the admission predicate fails three assertions, and breaking only the template still fails, so fold and template are independently covered. A real game was played to a promotion: the bare a7b8 now returns \"a promotion piece is required; use a7b8q, a7b8r, a7b8b, or a7b8n\", and a7b8q succeeds. Gates green including -race, each suite run twice. This is good work.\n\nNOW THE THING THAT CHANGES THE SHAPE OF YOUR FIX. Your assert framed the defect as a maximum-bound problem: at the host's string limit the derived key exceeds the bound. That is true, but the bound is only one trigger. The defect is that CreateNamed is not atomic at all.\n\nRead the function: Create commits durably, and then every subsequent failure path returns host.Record{} and discards created. That is four separate paths — the encode error, the Append error, the Decision error, and the not-found-or-not-effective branch — and each one throws away the identifier of a game that is already in the log. The maximum-bound case reaches the Append path; a transient failure of Append or Decision reaches the same place from a completely different direction.\n\nSo a fixed-size domain-separated key, which is what your assert prescribes and what my request repeated back to you, closes the trigger you found and leaves the defect standing for every other cause. I would rather tell you that now than have it come back in review. Please treat atomicity as the condition and key derivation as one part of it: either return created alongside the error so the caller learns the identifier, or treat a display-only naming failure as non-fatal and report it in the result, or preflight everything that can fail before the create commits. Your judgement which; the condition is that no path leaves a durable game whose identifier the caller never learns.\n\nWhy this matters more than it sounds. The game that lands is admission-open and joinable, the command exits non-zero, and the CLI prints nothing, so the user has no way to discover the identifier of a live game they just created. This was reproduced live, not reasoned about. And it is a regression on the exact command the first-user walkthrough opens with: the previous head created a named game in one atomic record, and splitting the name into a second act is what introduced the window.\n\nTHREE SMALLER ITEMS, none of which alone would block.\n\nTwo assertions in TestCreateV0JudgmentsRemainCompatibleInBothDirections are constants and can never fail. I checked this in the source: legacyName is initialised to the empty string and the loop only ever assigns legacyGameFound, so \"legacyName != \\\"\\\"\" is dead; and records[1] is the name record, so \"records[1].Schema == chess.SchemaCreate\" is dead too. The condition reduces to !legacyGameFound, which the table-driven case already covers. The comment above it claims the block verifies that a frozen v0 fold \"leaves the display-only name empty\", and it verifies nothing of the kind. That is worse than an absent test, because it reads as coverage. Cut it or make it assert the thing the comment promises. Related and worth stating: legacyDecision is a hand-rolled re-implementation of the v0 decoder living in the test file. It matches a1f130f's foldCreate today — that was checked by hand — but nothing keeps it accurate.\n\nThe fold-version bump is an undocumented breaking change. README has no mention of chess-fold@1, no mention that chess/name@0 is a separate durable act in the durable-rules list, and no note that existing repositories become unopenable; there is no rebind or migration path in host. I rate this low only on the assumption that the repo has no installed base yet. If that assumption is wrong, it is not low.\n\nAnd the lobby is the parallel place to the defect you already fixed. cmd/chess/ui/lobby.html renders \"White open · Black open\" for every game including restricted ones, and carries a static line telling the reader that for an open game they should have their agent use the join tool with the identifier. Weaker than the game-page defect because one is about seat emptiness and the other is a conditional the reader evaluates, but AdmissionOpen is already in scope for the lobby's loop, so saying it honestly there is a small change and closes the same class in the other place it lives.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT VERIFY. Neither page was rendered in a real browser. Every HTML conclusion — the escaping of a script-tag game name, and the presence or absence of the open-seat instruction across all three admission modes — comes from the served bytes and the test handler, not from observed rendering.\n\nOne residual I am explicitly not asking you to fix here: foldName passes an unvalidated body.Game into a refusal reason before checking it, so a refusal can carry attacker-chosen text. It matches the existing pattern in foldJoin and foldMove, so it is pre-existing rather than introduced by this head, and it belongs in its own request rather than riding along in a repair."}